import{_ as i,C as o,o as e,c as n,an as a,b as l,w as r,a as d,E as p,ao as c}from"./chunks/framework.BZkRsxf_.js";const _=JSON.parse('{"title":"","description":"","frontmatter":{"layout":"home","hero":{"name":"AtmosTransport.jl","tagline":"Mass-conserving offline atmospheric tracer transport on CPUs and GPUs.","image":{"src":"/logo.png","alt":"AtmosTransport"},"actions":[{"theme":"brand","text":"Get started","link":"/getting_started/installation"},{"theme":"alt","text":"Quickstart","link":"/getting_started/quickstart"},{"theme":"alt","text":"GitHub","link":"https://github.com/RemoteSensingTools/AtmosTransport.jl"}]}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"index.md","filePath":"index.md","lastUpdated":null}'),h={name:"index.md"};function g(u,t,m,y,f,b){const s=o("Mermaid");return e(),n("div",null,[t[1]||(t[1]=a(`<p>AtmosTransport transports atmospheric trace gases through prescribed meteorology. It supports regular latitude-longitude, reduced-Gaussian, and cubed-sphere grids; ERA5 and GEOS meteorology; and the same typed model on a CPU, NVIDIA GPU, or Apple GPU.</p><p>The package is under active development. It is a good fit for research and method development when you want the mass budget, preprocessing assumptions, and operator ordering to be explicit. It is not an online weather or climate model: winds and air-mass fluxes are prepared before the transport run.</p><h2 id="Your-first-successful-run" tabindex="-1">Your first successful run <a class="header-anchor" href="#Your-first-successful-run" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Your first successful run {#Your-first-successful-run}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>You do not need meteorological credentials, a GPU, or prior Julia experience. After <a href="/AtmosTransport.jl/dev/getting_started/installation#Installation">installing the repository</a>, two commands create a small current-format forcing file and run a four-hour CPU simulation:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> examples/generate_synthetic_quickstart.jl</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> scripts/run_transport.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> config/examples/minimal_template.toml</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The result is <code>data/quickstart/synthetic_output.nc</code>. The <a href="/AtmosTransport.jl/dev/getting_started/quickstart#Quickstart">Quickstart</a> explains every step and the <a href="/AtmosTransport.jl/dev/getting_started/julia_basics#Julia-orientation">Julia orientation</a> explains what <code>julia</code>, <code>--project=.</code>, and <code>using AtmosTransport</code> mean.</p><h2 id="The-model-in-one-picture" tabindex="-1">The model in one picture <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-model-in-one-picture" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The model in one picture {#The-model-in-one-picture}&quot;">​</a></h2>`,7)),(e(),l(c,null,{default:r(()=>[p(s,{id:"mermaid-19",class:"mermaid-diagram",graph:"flowchart%20LR%0A%20%20%20%20RAW%5BRaw%20meteorology%3Cbr%2F%3EERA5%20or%20GEOS%5D%0A%20%20%20%20PRE%5BPreprocessing%3Cbr%2F%3Ebalance%20and%20verify%5D%0A%20%20%20%20BIN%5BVersion-4%3Cbr%2F%3Etransport%20binary%5D%0A%20%20%20%20CFG%5BRun%20configuration%3Cbr%2F%3ETOML%5D%0A%20%20%20%20RUN%5BTyped%20runtime%3Cbr%2F%3Egrid%20%2B%20state%20%2B%20operators%5D%0A%20%20%20%20OUT%5BSnapshots%3Cbr%2F%3ENetCDF%20or%20ATMSNAP%5D%0A%20%20%20%20RAW%20--%3E%20PRE%20--%3E%20BIN%20--%3E%20RUN%20--%3E%20OUT%0A%20%20%20%20CFG%20--%3E%20RUN%0A"})]),fallback:r(()=>[...t[0]||(t[0]=[d(" Loading... ",-1)])]),_:1})),t[2]||(t[2]=a('<p>Preprocessing owns data conversion, grid transforms, dry-air bookkeeping, and continuity checks. The runtime memory-maps the resulting binary, constructs the correct grid and operators from its header plus a TOML file, and advances one meteorological window at a time. See <a href="/AtmosTransport.jl/dev/concepts/architecture#Architecture-tour">Architecture tour</a> for the objects and source directories behind this flow.</p><h2 id="Choose-a-path" tabindex="-1">Choose a path <a class="header-anchor" href="#Choose-a-path" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Choose a path {#Choose-a-path}&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">If you want to…</th><th style="text-align:right;">Start with…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Try the model on any laptop</td><td style="text-align:right;"><a href="/AtmosTransport.jl/dev/getting_started/installation#Installation">Installation</a> → <a href="/AtmosTransport.jl/dev/getting_started/quickstart#Quickstart">Quickstart</a></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Learn just enough Julia to follow examples</td><td style="text-align:right;"><a href="/AtmosTransport.jl/dev/getting_started/julia_basics#Julia-orientation">Julia orientation</a></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Run with your own ERA5 or GEOS data</td><td style="text-align:right;"><a href="/AtmosTransport.jl/dev/getting_started/first_run#Run-with-real-meteorology">Run with real meteorology</a> → <a href="/AtmosTransport.jl/dev/preprocessing/overview#Preprocessing-overview">Preprocessing overview</a></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Understand mass, grids, and physics choices</td><td style="text-align:right;"><a href="/AtmosTransport.jl/dev/concepts/architecture#Architecture-tour">Architecture tour</a> → <a href="/AtmosTransport.jl/dev/concepts/state_and_basis#State-and-basis">State &amp; basis</a> → <a href="/AtmosTransport.jl/dev/concepts/operators#Operator-concepts">Operators</a></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Map familiar TM5/GCHP concepts onto this code</td><td style="text-align:right;"><a href="/AtmosTransport.jl/dev/for_tm5_gchp_users/philosophy#Design-philosophy">Design philosophy</a></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Extend or call the library directly</td><td style="text-align:right;"><a href="/AtmosTransport.jl/dev/api/public_api#Curated-public-API">Curated public API</a> and the generated API pages</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="Core-capabilities" tabindex="-1">Core capabilities <a class="header-anchor" href="#Core-capabilities" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Core capabilities {#Core-capabilities}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><p><strong>Mass-conserving transport.</strong> Air mass and conservative tracer storage follow the same discrete fluxes, with write-time replay and positivity gates.</p></li><li><p><strong>Several horizontal topologies.</strong> Lat-lon, reduced Gaussian, and cubed-sphere use one public driver and model interface.</p></li><li><p><strong>Composable physics.</strong> Advection, vertical diffusion, convection, surface fluxes, and simple chemistry are typed operators selected at configuration time.</p></li><li><p><strong>Portable execution.</strong> CPU is the simplest starting point; CUDA and Metal are optional backends. Metal uses <code>Float32</code>.</p></li><li><p><strong>Explicit provenance.</strong> Only transport-binary format version 4 is accepted; incompatible forcing fails at load time rather than being guessed.</p></li></ul><h2 id="Where-things-live" tabindex="-1">Where things live <a class="header-anchor" href="#Where-things-live" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where things live {#Where-things-live}&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Repository path</th><th style="text-align:right;">Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>examples/</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Small runnable examples that need no external data.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>config/examples/</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Canonical, copyable TOML run templates.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>scripts/run_transport.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">One command-line entry point for simulations.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>scripts/preprocessing/preprocess_transport_binary.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">One command-line entry point for meteorology preprocessing.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>src/</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Package implementation, organized by grids, state, operators, drivers, models, preprocessing, and output.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>test/core/</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Synthetic regression tests that do not require private datasets.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The top-level repository <code>README.md</code> carries the current capability status. These pages provide the learning path, workflows, theory, and generated API.</p>',8))])}const v=i(h,[["render",g]]);export{_ as __pageData,v as default};
